From: Prabhsimran Singh <prabhsimrans@nvidia.com>
Subject: ggml-cuda: sparse flash attention over per-sequence cell lists

Builds on the sparse flash-attention path:

- The mask columns of the selected KV cells are compacted per query group, so
  the MMA kernel reads only those columns.
- For a metadata-only mask (ggml_build_kq_mask_meta) the indices, counts and
  compact mask come straight from the per-sequence cell lists; no dense
  [n_kv, n_tokens] mask is materialized. Other kernels get a dense mask built
  on the fly. The index build splits each list into 2,048-slot chunks over a
  2-D grid and pads only to the next multiple of 256 (index -1, mask -inf).
- Sparse configs for DKQ = DV = 128 with 8-wide GQA (Nemotron-H), and a
  <128,128,1,16> instance for one query row per sequence (the chained MTP-head
  drafts): it reads each sequence's list once per KV head, where 8 x 8 tiles
  read it twice. Op param 2 of the metadata mask gives the query rows per
  sequence (0 if unknown).
- Stream-K splits the tiles' real 64-row batches evenly (a one-block prefix
  kernel counts them, a matching fixup combines the pieces) instead of giving
  every tile the span of its list capacity, and a block whose share starts past
  a tile's list skips it. The gather for head size 128 pipelines its loads with
  two cp.async stages like the dense kernel.
- Backends with serving.fused skip stream-K for short KV spans whose output
  tiles already fill the GPU (encoder windows).

From cell lists a tile gathers only the cells of its own sequences and stops
at their count, a subset of the occupied KV span that the dense kernel reads
for every tile. For list masks the gather is therefore chosen once the span
holds two of the longest sequences; dense masks, gathered at their full width,
keep the bound of twice the gather buffer width (ncols1 x the longest
sequence).

The gather buffer of a list mask holds ncols1 x the per-sequence bound slots
per tile and is never bounded by the K/V span: a cell shared by several
sequences (a prompt prefix copied with seq_cp) is listed once per sequence, so
on a nearly full pool a tile's lists can hold more entries than the span, and
a span-bounded buffer would drop keys of the tile's last sequence. Dense masks
keep the span bound.

Upstream: the compact mask, the chunked list gather and the stream-K split over
real batches are candidates with the sparse path; the cell-list mask and its
one-row instance are NeMo-specific.

